0038 | \begin{abstract}
0039 | Suppose that $E$ is an elliptic curve defined over $\mathbb{Q}$ without complex multiplication and with conductor $N$. For each positive integer $m$, the action of the absolute Galois group $G_{\mathbb{Q}}=\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ on the torsion points over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ gives rise to a representation of $G_\mathbb{Q}$. A celebrated paper of Serre shows that this representation is surjective for all sufficiently large primes; the other primes are termed \emph{exceptional}.
0040 | Serre conjectures that there are no exceptional primes $\ell>37$ for any non CM elliptic curve over $\mathbb{Q}$. The best result in this direction is due to Cojocaru, who proves that the largest exceptional prime $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon} N^{1+\epsilon}$. In this paper we lower the exponent on the bound to obtain $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon} N_0^{\frac{1}{4}+\epsilon}$, where $N_0$ is the product of primes of bad reduction. If $E$ has no places of multiplicative reduction, then we have $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N^{\frac{1}{8}+\epsilon}$. Assuming the Frey-Szpiro conjecture, we have that  $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N^{\frac{1}{8}+\epsilon}$ in general. Our main methods include the Rankin-Selberg method and the classical work on distribution of quadratic residues.
0041 | \end{abstract}
0042 | \section{Introduction and Statement of Results}
0043 | \indent
0044 | Let $E/\mathbb{Q}$ be an elliptic curve defined over $\mathbb{Q}$. The rational points $E(\mathbb{Q})$ form an abelian group in a natural way, and a fundamental result of Mordell says that this group is finitely generated. In other words, $E(\mathbb{Q})\cong E(\mathbb{Q})^{\operatorname{tors}}\times \mathbb{Z}^r$ for some non-negative integer $r$ which we call the \emph{rank}. Determining the torsion of this group for a given curve is relatively easy, and Mazur showed that it is one of 16 simple possibilities. Calculating the rank is much more difficult. At present, no effective method for computing $r$ is known to unconditionally work. Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer have conjectured, however, that this information is encoded in the $\ell$-adic representations attached to $E$.
0045 | \\
0046 | \indent
0047 | To be more specific, recall that over a fixed algebraic closure $\mathbb{Q}$ and for $n\geq 1$, we have an isomorphism of groups $E[\overline{\mathbb{Q}}][\ell^n]\simeq(\mathbb{Z}/\ell^n\mathbb{Z})^2$, where $E(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})[\ell^n]$ is the group of $p^n$ torsion points over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Now $G_{\mathbb{Q}}$ acts on the left side via the finite Galois group quotient $\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}(E[\ell^n])/\mathbb{Q})$. The isomorphism gives a representation $\phi_{E,\ell^n}\colon G_{\mathbb{Q}}\rightarrow \operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell^n\mathbb{Z})$. It is often more useful to work over rings of characteristic $0$, so one can consider the \emph{Tate module} $T_\ell(E):=\varprojlim E(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})[\ell^n]\simeq\mathbb{Z}_\ell^2$. This gives rise to a continuous representation $\rho_{\ell}:G_{\mathbb{Q}}\rightarrow\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}_p)$. Now set $a_{\ell}:=\ell+1-\# E[\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z}]$. Then the famous conjecture of Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer  predicts that the rank is encoded in the behavior at $s=1$ of the Euler product \begin{equation} L_{\text{good}}(E,s):=\displaystyle\prod_{\text{good }\ell}(1-a_{\ell}\ell^{-s}+\ell^{1-2s}).\end{equation} For each $\ell$, the Galois representation $\rho_{\ell}$ encodes the information of all Euler factors at primes $p\neq\ell$.
0048 | \\
0049 | \indent
0050 | We have the following deep theorem of Serre on the nature of these Galois representations \cite{Serre1}:
0051 | \begin{thm*}(Serre Open Image Theorem, 1972)
0052 | Suppose $E$ is an elliptic curve over $\mathbb{Q}$ without complex multiplication. Then the $\ell$-adic representaion has finite index (equivalently open image) in $\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}_{\ell})$. Furthermore, there are finitely many exceptional primes.
0053 | \end{thm*}
0054 | \begin{rmk}
0055 | If $E$ has CM, the representation is never surjective, except for possibly when $\ell=2$.
0056 | \end{rmk}
0057 | Serre's theorem has inspired research in various directions. For example, Duke studies families of curves and proves that ``almost all'' non-CM curves have no exceptional primes (when counted by height) \cite{Duke}. Other papers address the index of the adelic representation, obtained by patching together the $\ell$-adic representations for each prime. For example, Zywina finds effective bounds on this index \cite{Zywina} and Greicius finds necessary and sufficient conditions for it to be surjective \cite{Greicius}, using these to compute explicit examples.
0058 | \\
0059 | \indent
0060 | A natural question asks how large the exceptional primes can be. If $E$ is semistable, Mazur showed in 1978 that there are no exceptional primes $\ell>11$ \cite{Mazur}. For general non-CM curves this is false. However, there is strong heuristic evidence for the following:
0061 | \begin{conjec}[Serre]
0062 | Every prime $\ell>37$ is not exceptional for any elliptic curve $E/\mathbb{Q}$ without CM.\end{conjec}
0063 | In this direction, let $\ell_0$ be the largest exceptional prime of $E$. Serre showed in \cite{Serre2} that, assuming GRH for the Dedekind Zeta functions, \begin{equation}\ell_0\leq C(\log N)(\log\log 2N)^3\ \ \text{(GRH)}\end{equation} for some positive absolute constant $C$. The first unconditional bound was given by Masser and W\"{u}stholz in terms of the Faltings height $h(E)$ and an effective (but huge) constant $\gamma$:
0064 | \begin{equation} \ell_0\leq C h(E)^{\gamma},\end{equation} for an absolute constant $C$. The best current result in terms of the conductor of $E$ is due to Cojocaru \cite{Cojocaru} and is similar to a previous estimate of Kraus \cite{Kraus}. She shows that \begin{equation} \ell_0\leq\frac{4\sqrt{6}}{3}N\displaystyle\prod_{p\vert N}\left(1+\frac{1}{p}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+1\ll_{\epsilon} N^{1+\epsilon}.\end{equation} These two estimates are related by the following conjecture of Frey and Szpiro, which is equivalent to the abc conjecture.
0065 | \begin{conjec}(Frey-Szpiro)
0066 | \begin{enumerate}
0067 | \item For any elliptic curve $E/\mathbb{Q}$ and any $\epsilon>0$, there exists a constant $C_{\epsilon}$ such that $h(E)\leq(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon)\log N+C_{\epsilon}$.
0068 | \item For any elliptic curve $E/\mathbb{Q}$ and any $\epsilon>0$, there exists a constant $C_{\epsilon}$ such that $\Delta\ll_{\epsilon} N^{6+\epsilon}$, where $\Delta$ is the minimal discriminant of $E$ and $N$ is the conductor.
0069 | \end{enumerate}
0070 | \end{conjec}
0071 | In this paper, we improve Cojocaru's bound:
0072 | \begin{thm}
0073 | Let $E$ be an elliptic curve over $\mathbb{Q}$ without complex multiplication. Let $\ell_0$ be the largest exceptional prime of $E$ and $N_0:=\displaystyle\prod_{\operatorname{primes }p\vert N} p$. Then for any $\epsilon>0$ we have \[\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N_0^{\frac{1}{4}+\epsilon}.\]
0074 | \end{thm}
0075 | Now we state a few comments on the main theorem.
0076 | \begin{rmk}[1]
0077 | It is likely that the exponent in this bound can be improved slightly using the subconvexity bounds for Rankin-Selberg $L$-functions due to Michel and others \cite{Michel}
0078 | \end{rmk}
0079 | \begin{rmk}[2]
0080 | This theorem has immediate applications to other results. For example, it is clear from the proof that the index bounds in Theorem 1.1 of Zywina \cite{Zywina} are made sharper using Theorem 1.1 above.
0081 | \end{rmk}
0082 | We will also show the following.
0083 | \begin{thm}
0084 | Let $E$ be as in Theorem 1.1. If $E$ has no primes of multiplicative reduction, then $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N^{\frac{1}{8}+\epsilon}$. If there is a prime of multiplicative reduction, then $\ell_0\leq\log\Delta$, where $\Delta$ is the minimal discriminant of $E$.
0085 | \end{thm}
0086 | In practice, we have that $\log\Delta$ is much smaller than $N^{\frac{1}{4}}$, and the case of no multiplicative reduction is most difficult to compute. Unconditionally, we have the following corollary.
0087 | \begin{corollary}
0088 | Assuming the truth of the Frey-Szpiro Conjecture (2), we have that $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N^{\frac{1}{8}+\epsilon}$ for all non-CM elliptic curves $E/\mathbb{Q}$.
0089 | \end{corollary}
0090 | The paper is organized as follows. In $\S2$, we recall a criterion of Serre for surjective representations. We then review the classical Rankin-Selberg method and character sums. Using these along with the Modularity Theorem and Deligne bound, we prove the theorem in $\S3$.
0091 | \section{Nuts and Bolts}
0092 | In this section we outline the basic theory necessary to prove Theorem 1.1, starting with a criterion of Serre on the possible failure of a representation to be surjective.
0093 | \subsection{Serre's Criterion}
0094 | Recall the following classification due to Serre of maximal subgroups in his 1981 paper. Let $G_{\ell}$ be the image of $\phi_{\ell}$ and $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ be the image of $G_{\ell}$ in $\operatorname{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z}):=\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})/(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})^{\ast}$. Then we have the following:
0095 | \begin{thm*}[Serre \cite{Serre2}]
0096 | Suppose $\ell$ is exceptional. Then one of the following four cases holds:
0097 | \begin{itemize}
0098 | \item $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ is contained in a Borel subgroup of $ \operatorname{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})$;
0099 | \item $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ is contained in a non-split Cartan subgroup of $\operatorname{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})$;
0100 | \item $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ is contained isomorphic to one of the permutation groups $A_4, S_4,\text{ or }A_5$;
0101 | \item $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ is contained in the normalizer $N_{\ell}$ of a Cartan subgroup $C_{\ell}$ of $\operatorname{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})$ but not contained in $C_{\ell}$.
0102 | \end{itemize}
0103 | \end{thm*}
0104 | In the same paper, Serre also showed that the first three cannot happen if $\ell>37$. In the fourth case, the index of $C_{\ell}$ in $N_{\ell}$ must be 2, and we let $\epsilon_{\ell}$ denote the composition \[G_{\mathbb{Q}}\rightarrow G_{\ell}\rightarrow N_{\ell}/C_{\ell}\rightarrow\{\pm1\}.\] Now $\epsilon_{\ell}$ is a quadratic character of $G_{\mathbb{Q}}$ which can be identified with a quadratic Dirichlet character. Serre showed that this character must satisfy two special properties:
0105 | \begin{prop}
0106 | Suppose $\hat{G_{\ell}}$ is contained in the normalizer $N_{\ell}$ of a Cartan subgroup $C_{\ell}$ of $\operatorname{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})$ but not contained in $C_{\ell}$. Then we have:\end{prop}
0107 | \begin{enumerate}
0108 | \item $\epsilon_{\ell}$ \emph{is unramified at primes not dividing} $N$, \emph{the conductor of} $E$;
0109 | \item $\ell \vert a_p$ \emph{for any prime} $p\not\vert N$ \emph{such that} $\epsilon_{\ell}(p)=-1$.
0110 | \end{enumerate}
0111 | 
0112 | We will use  the modularity of elliptic curves over $\mathbb{Q}$ proven by Wiles, Taylor, Diamond, et. al. One consequence of this result is that to any elliptic curve $E/\mathbb{Q}$, we can attach a Hecke eigenform of weight 2 which is a newform of level $N$, the conductor of $E$. We recall the bound on coefficients proven by Deligne:
0113 | \begin{thm*}
0114 | Let $f=\displaystyle\sum a_nq^n$ be a cuspform. Let $d(n)$ be the divisor function. Then $\vert a_n\vert\leq d(n)p^{\frac{k-1}{2}}$ for all positive integers $n$.
0115 | \end{thm*}
0116 | Now for $f(z)=\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_nq^n$, define the quadratic twist of $f$ by the quadratic Dirichlet character $\chi$ by the series $\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\chi(n)a_nq^n$. The key result is the following theorem (see \cite{Ono}):
0117 | \begin{prop}
0118 | The twist $f^{\prime}$ of a cusp form $f\in S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$ by a quadratic character $\chi$ of conductor $r$ is a cusp form in $S_k(\Gamma_0(M))$ where $M=\operatorname{lcm}(N,r^2)$.
0119 | \end{prop}
0120 |  Let $f$ be the modular form attached to $E$. We would like to find a small prime $p$ of good reduction such that $a_p\neq 0$ and $\epsilon_{\ell}(p)=-1$.  It is easy to see that $f\neq f^{\prime}$. For otherwise every prime with $\epsilon_{\ell}(p)=-1$ has $a_p=0$. This is a contradiction as by Dirichlet's theorem the number of primes $p\leq x$ with $\epsilon_{\ell}(p)=-1$ is asymptotic to $\frac{x}{2\log x}$ but by Serre's same 1981 paper \cite{Serre2}, the number of primes $p\leq x$ for which $a_p=0$ for any non-CM elliptic curve is $o(\frac{x}{\log x})$.
0121 | \\
0122 | \indent
0123 | For convenience, we twist again by a quadratic character vanishing only at the primes of bad reduction of $E$ to obtain forms $\tilde{f}$ and $\tilde{f}^{\prime}$ in $S_2(\Gamma_0(M))$ where $M\vert 16N^2$ which vanish at all primes of bad reduction of $E$. By the same argument, we have that $\tilde{f}\neq\tilde{f}^{\prime}$. Suppose that we could find a small positive integer $n$ for which $\tilde{a}_n\neq\tilde{a}^{\prime}_n$. It follows then that $a_n\neq a_n^{\prime}$ and $(n,N)=1$, so $a_n\neq 0$. Now we use the Hecke eigenform relations of $f$ and $f^{\prime}$ to find a small prime $p$ satisfying these conditions. By multiplicivity of coefficients, there exists $p\vert n$ such that $a_{p^k}\neq a_{p^k}^{\prime}$ where $p^k\vert\vert n$. The recursion relations for prime powers of eigenforms futher give us that $0\neq a_p\neq a_p^{\prime}$. Thus, as $(p,N)=1$ and $\epsilon_{\ell}$ is unramified outside $N$, $\epsilon_{\ell}(p)=-1$. By the divisibility condition $(2)$ of Proposition 1 and the Deligne bound, it follows that \begin{equation}\ell\leq a_p\leq 2\sqrt{p}.\end{equation}
0124 | \\
0125 | \indent
0126 | As $\epsilon_{\ell}$ is unramified outside $N_0$, the conductor $q$ of $\epsilon_{\ell}$ is satisfies $q\leq 4N_0$. To prove Theorem 1.1, we will first prove the following lemma, which implies that the bound on $\ell_0$ is $\ll N_0^{\frac{1}{4}+\epsilon}$, where $N_0:=\displaystyle\prod_{p\vert N} p$:
0127 | \begin{lemm}
0128 | Let $f$ be a normalized newform of weight $k$ and level $N$, $\chi$ a quadratic Dirichlet character modulo $q$, and $f^{\prime}$ the twist of $f$ by $\chi$. Assume $N$ is squarefree, $f$ is attached to an elliptic curve without CM, or $f$ is a quadratic twist of a form of one of these two types. Then for any $\epsilon>0$, there is a constant $C$ depending only on $\epsilon$ such that there exists $n\leq Cq^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon}$ for which $a_f(n)\neq a_{f^{\prime}}(n)$.
0129 | \end{lemm}
0130 | The proof of the theorem will then follow from a Tate curve argument sketched below.
0131 | \subsection{The Rankin-Selberg Method}
0132 | The Rankin-Selberg method utilizes properties and integral representations of $L$-functions constructed as a convolution of two modular forms $f$ and $g$. These were originally used by Rankin to study sums of squares of coefficients of a modular form. To define this convolution, let $f\in S_k(q,\chi_q)^{\ast}$ and $g\in S_{k^{\prime}}(D,\chi_D)^{\ast}$ be normalized newforms for Dirichlet characters mod $q$ and $D$ respectively. We will define the \emph{normalized eigenvalues} of a newform $\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_nq^n$ by $\lambda_nn^{\frac{k-1}{2}}=a_n$, so that the Deligne bound becomes \begin{equation}\lambda_n\leq d(n).\end{equation} Setting $\chi:=\chi_q\chi_D$, we define the Rankin-Selberg convolution by the $L$-series with Euler product \begin{equation}L(f\otimes g,s):=L(\chi,2s)\displaystyle\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{\lambda_f(n)\lambda_g(n)}{n^s}=\displaystyle\prod_p\prod_{i=1}^2\prod_{j=1}^2(1-\frac{a_{f,i}(p)a_{g,j}(p)}{p^s})^{-1},\end{equation} where for each prime $p$, $a_{f,1}(p), a_{f,2}(p)$, and $a_{g,1}(p), a_{g,2}(p)$ are the roots of the quadratic equations \begin{equation}X^2-\lambda_f(p)X+\chi_q(p)=0,\ \ X^2-\lambda_g(p)X+\chi_D(p)=0.\end{equation}
0133 | Rankin, Selberg, and others showed that $L(f\otimes g,s)$ admits a holomorphic analytic continuation to the entire complex plane unless $f=\overline{g}$, in which case there are simple poles at $s=0$ and $s=1$. Moreover, it admits an integral representation and hence a functional equation relating $s$ to $1-s$. A key fact when $f=\overline{g}$ is the following: \begin{prop}The symmetric square $L(f\otimes \overline{f},s)$ has a simple pole at $s=1$ with residue \begin{equation}\operatorname{res}(L(f\otimes \overline{f},s))_{s=1}:=r=\frac{12(4\pi)^{k-1}\langle f,\overline{f}\rangle}{N(k-1)!\displaystyle\prod_{p\vert N}(1-\frac{1}{p})}.\end{equation}\end{prop}
0134 | \subsection{An Inequality of P\'{o}lya-Vinogradov}
0135 | 
0136 | An important problem in analytic number theory is to estimate the distribution of sets of numbers such as primes or quadratic residues in various intervals. A natural question asks how large the smallest quadratic residue modulo $n$ can be. One way to control this behavior is by estimating character sums.
0137 | \\
0138 | \indent
0139 | For a quadratic Dirichlet character $\chi$ of conductor $q$, one would like to bound $\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq x}\chi(n)$. A classical inequality of P\`{o}lya and Vinogradov states: \begin{prop}For a non-principal Dirichlet character of conductor $q$, and for all positive integers $x$, \begin{equation}\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq x}\chi(n)\ll\sqrt{q}\log q.\end{equation}\end{prop} In fact, to be explicit, we can write \begin{equation}\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq z}\chi(n)\leq 2\sqrt{q}\log q.\end{equation}
0140 | \section{Proof of Theorems 1.1 and 1.2}
0141 | We are now in position to prove Lemma 1. Let $X$ be the largest integer such that $\lambda_f(n)=\lambda_{f^{\prime}}(n)$ for all $n\leq X$. By Proposition 3, the symmetric square $L(f\otimes f,s)$ has a simple pole at $s=1$, and so \begin{equation}X\ll_f\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}\vert\lambda_f(n)\vert^2=\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}\lambda_f(n)\lambda_{f^{\prime}}(n)=\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}\lambda_f(n)^2\chi(n).\end{equation}
0142 | We can choose the implied constant in (11) to be $\frac{1}{r}$ where $r$ is the residue given in Proposition 3. We then use a lower bound on $\langle f,f\rangle$. For the square-free case, this is a theorem of Hoffstein and Lockhart \cite{Hoffstein-Lockhart}.
0143 |  \begin{thm*}
0144 |  Let $f$ be a normalized newform of weight $k$ and square-free level $N$. Then there exists an effective constant $C$ depending only on $k$ such that \begin{equation}\langle f,f\rangle\geq C\frac{N}{\log N}.\end{equation}
0145 |  \end{thm*}
0146 |  Plugging this inequality in, the two factors of $N$ cancel and we can write the implied constant in (11) as a function of $N$ which is $\ll N^{\epsilon}$. This bound (12) is in fact a basic corollary of the following result of Goldfeld, Hoffstein, and Liemann.
0147 | \begin{prop*}
0148 | Let $f$ be a normalized newform of weight $k$ and square-free level $N$. Then there exists an absolute constant $C$ such that $L(\operatorname{Sym}^2f,s)$ has no zero in the region \[\sigma\geq 1-\frac{C}{\log kN}.\]
0149 | \end{prop*}
0150 |  Such zero-free regions are also known to exist for the case of non-CM elliptic curves. For example, we have the following result of Watkins \cite{Watkins}.
0151 |  \begin{thm*}
0152 |  Let $E/\mathbb{Q}$ be an elliptic curve without CM of conductor $N$ and $f$ the associated weight 2 newform. Then $L(\operatorname{Sym}^2f,s)$ has no zeros with \[\sigma\geq 1-\frac{C}{\log (N^{(2)}/D)}.\]
0153 |  \end{thm*}
0154 |  Here $N^{(2)}$ is the symmetric-square conductor of $E$. It is easy to find a constant to modify the proof of Hoffstein and Lockhart using the fact that $N^{(2)}\leq N^2$. Note that we are actually studying the zero-free region associated to the quadratic twist of a newform coming from an elliptic curve (i.e., $\tilde{f}$), but this doesn't affect the result as $\chi^2=1$.
0155 |  \\
0156 |  \indent
0157 | Now use the Deligne bound (6) and the classical fact that $d(n)\ll n^{\epsilon}$ for any $\epsilon>0$ to conclude \begin{equation}\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}\lambda_f(n)^2\chi(n)\ll_{\epsilon}\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}n^{2\epsilon}\chi(n)\leq X^{2\epsilon}\displaystyle\sum_{n\leq X}\chi(n)\leq  2X^{2\epsilon}\sqrt{q}\log q.\end{equation}
0158 | Combining (11) and (13) gives \begin{equation} X\ll_{\epsilon} q^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon},\end{equation} which is Lemma 1, where the second inequality follows as by definition $\chi(n)$ is nonnegative for $n\leq X$. By Proposition 1 and the resulting inequality (5), we have proven Theorem 1.1.\\ \indent Now we turn to the proof of Theorem 1.2.
0159 | If there are no primes of multiplicative reduction, then we have $N_0^2\leq N$ (they may not be equal depending on reduction at the $p=2,3$). In this case,  $\ell_0\ll_{\epsilon}N^{\frac{1}{8}+\epsilon}$. If there is a prime of multiplicative reduction, then in fact the bound on exceptional primes can be (conjecturally and in practice) improved by the following argument due to Zywina.
0160 | \begin{lemm}
0161 | Let $E/\mathbb{Q}$ be an elliptic curve without CM and with conductor $N$. Suppose that there is a prime $p$ of multiplicative reduction. Then $\rho_{E,\ell}(G_{\mathbb{Q}})=\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{F}_{\ell})$ for all $\ell\gg \log\Delta$.
0162 | \end{lemm}
0163 | \begin{proof}
0164 | We may assume that $\ell>37$. The $j$-invariant of $E$ is $j=c_4^3/\Delta$ where $\Delta$ is the discriminant of the \emph{minimal model} of $E$. That $p$ is a prime of multiplicative reduction means that $\operatorname{ord}_p(c_4)=0$ and $\operatorname{ord}_p(\Delta)>0$. Define $n:=-\operatorname{ord}_p(j)=\operatorname{ord}_p(\Delta)$, which will be positive. Since $\Delta$ is the discriminant of the minimal model of $E$, the primes $q$ that divide $\Delta$ are those of bad reduction. Thus, \begin{equation} n=\operatorname{ord}_p(\Delta)\leq\log\Delta/\log p\leq\log\Delta.\end{equation} If $\ell$ is a prime greater than $n$, then $\rho_{E,\ell}(G_{\mathbb{Q}})$ contains an element conjugate to the matrix \[\begin{pmatrix} 1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.\] For the proof of this fact, see \cite{SerreAbelRep} IV-20 $\S3.2$. The proof uses that $E$ over $\mathbb{Q}_p$ is isomorphic to a twist of a Tate curve; since $\ell>2$, there is no harm in working with the twist to construct the specific matrix. By Serre's criterion in $\S2.1$, as $\ell>37$, the image of $\rho_{E,\ell}$ must be contained in the normalizer of a Cartan subgroup. Since $\ell>2$, the normalizer of a Cartan subgroup does not contain an element of order $\ell$; thus $\rho_{E,\ell}(G_{\mathbb{Q}})=\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{F}_{\ell})$.
0165 | \\
0166 | Therefore, if $\ell\geq \log\Delta$, we find that $\ell>n$ by (16) and hence $\rho_{E,\ell}(G_{\mathbb{Q}})=\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb{F}_{\ell})$.
0167 | \end{proof}
